.TH GREP 1
.SH NAME
grep, egrep, fgrep \- search a file for a pattern
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B grep
[ option ] ... expression [ file ] ...
.PP
.B egrep
[ option ] ... [ expression ] [ file ] ...
.PP
.B fgrep
[ option ] ... [ strings ] [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Commands of the
.B grep
family search the input
.I files
(standard input default) for lines matching a pattern.
Normally, each line found is copied to the standard output; unless the
.B \-h
flag is used, the file name is shown if there is more than one input file.
.PP
.B grep
patterns are limited regular expressions in the style of
.BR ed (1);
it uses a compact nondeterministic algorithm.
.B egrep
patterns are full regular expressions; it uses a fast deterministic algorithm that sometimes needs exponential space.
.B fgrep
patterns are fixed strings; it is fast and compact.
To determine which variation to use, match the complexity of the search expression and the speed in which it can be done.
.PP
The following options are recognized.
.TP 8
.B \-v
All lines but those matching are printed.
.TP
.B \-c
Only a count of matching lines is printed.
.TP
.B \-l
The names of files with matching lines are listed (once) separated by newlines.
.TP
.B \-n
Each line is preceded by its line number in the file.
.TP
.B \-b
Each line is preceded by the block number on which it was found.
This is sometimes useful in locating disk block numbers by context.
.TP
.B \-s
No output is produced, only status.
.TP
.B \-h
Do not print filename headers with output lines.
.TP
.B \-y
Lower case letters in the pattern will also match upper case letters in the input
.RB ( grep
only).
.TP
.BI \-e " expression"
Same as a simple
.I expression
argument, but useful when the
.I expression
begins with a
.BR \- .
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
The regular expression
.RB ( egrep )
or string list
.RB ( fgrep )
is taken from the
.IR file .
This does not work for
.BR grep .
.TP
.B \-x
(Exact) only lines matched in their entirety are printed
.RB ( fgrep
only).
Single quotes should be used for multiple word expressions.
.PP
Care should be taken when using the characters
\fB$ * [ ^ | ? ' " ( )\fR
and
.B \e
in the
.I expression
as they are also meaningful to the Shell.
It is safest to enclose the entire
.I expression
argument in single quotes `' '.
For example:
.RS
.nf
grep \-n '/*' *.c
.fi
.RE
prints out all comment lines in all C source files in the current directory.
.PP
.B fgrep
searches for lines that contain one of the (newline-separated)
.IR strings .
.PP
.B egrep
accepts extended regular expressions.
In the following description `character' excludes newline:
.RS
.PP
A
.B \e
followed by a single character matches that character.
.PP
The character
.B ^
.RB ( $ )
matches the beginning (end) of a line.
.PP
A
.B .
matches any character.
.PP
A single character not otherwise endowed with special meaning matches that character.
.PP
A string enclosed in brackets
.B [ ]
matches any single character from the string.
Ranges of ASCII character codes may be abbreviated as in `a\-z0\-9'.
A
.B ]
may occur only as the first character of the string.
A literal
.B \-
must be placed where it can't be mistaken as a range indicator.
.PP
A regular expression followed by
.B *
.RB ( + ,
.BR ? )
matches a sequence of 0 or more (1 or more, 0 or 1) matches of the regular expression.
.PP
Two regular expressions concatenated match a match of the first followed by a match of the second.
.PP
Two regular expressions separated by
.B |
or newline match either a match for the first or a match for the second.
.PP
A regular expression enclosed in parentheses matches a match for the regular expression.
.RE
.PP
The order of precedence of operators at the same parenthesis level is
.B [ ]
then
.B "* + ?"
then concatenation then
.B |
and newline.
.SH EXAMPLES
Search for all occurrences of the string ``xyz'' in file
.I prog.c
.RS
.nf
grep xyz prog.c
.fi
.RE
Search the file
.I history.text
for the words given in file
.IR spellerrs .
.RS
.nf
fgrep \-f spellerrs history.text
.fi
.RE
Count the number of lines with the words ``parrot'' or ``Parrot'':
.RS
.nf
grep \-c '[Pp]arrot' prog.c
.fi
.RE
(note the use of single quotes to prevent the shell from interpreting the left and right brackets).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ed(1), sed(1), sh(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 if any matches are found, 1 if none, 2 for syntax errors or inaccessible files.
.SH BUGS
Lines are limited to 256 characters; longer lines are truncated.
.sp
